#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128831 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "323261a0-d9c6-587b-93bf-b2e831893af5" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3479" :question/problem "The point $P$ is located in the second quadrant and lies on the unit circle.\nThe $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}$. What is the $x$-coordinate of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\n$$\n\ninto the above and solving for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 1 - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx^{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\\nx &= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9089ba5c-f241-574e-9592-a26e09535b69" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "a8e5384a-5baa-537f-9ec0-c775026f98d5" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3481" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{3}{4}$, what is the value of $\\cos \\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2021/Source/Images/8b6363ef3d603f43d9169280502749cc.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^{2} θ &= 1 - \\sin^{2} θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{3}{4})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{9}{16}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt {7}}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "98d6004a-547a-5b0b-9748-9571cc3ee632" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "97f55dee-3f6b-5c1f-a901-b391ab37a0df" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3483" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that $\\sin\\theta=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, what is the value of $\\tan\\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2021/Source/Images/6e4741d820efa51e7f82e4abc1c518b4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\cos^{2} θ &= 1 - \\sin^{2} θ \\\\\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\sin^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\cos\\theta$ is positive in the fourth quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nFinally, knowing that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-\\sqrt 3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128834 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a09362-632a-56c4-b246-903e18fc77ee" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "cfe7b419-9067-5b8c-b9c2-d4ec91120333" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3486" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}$, what is $\\csc\\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2021/Source/Images/7203bdaa2af0eb3b29f22c170b3c88e5.png)" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{3}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is negative in the third quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = -\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nFinally, knowing that\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99770af6-3f14-5f15-9c64-adaa0914065e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{\\sqrt{31}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126804} {:answer/id #uuid "cb71c16a-93cd-51f7-8735-bbcce913df55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 175921861